Key insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="3ad05f52" lines="128-142">
<description>
Paint the maze corridor and framed rooms lying on shortest paths between the two colored endpoint components.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background, one wall color forming thin orthogonal frames, and one endpoint/fill color.
- The endpoint/fill color forms exactly two 4-connected solid rectangular components; their larger side lengths define the framed room sizes.

Rule
- Treat wall pixels as blocked and every other pixel as passable.
- Find every passable pixel that lies on a shortest 4-connected path between the two endpoint components.
- Also fill any framed square room of an endpoint-derived size that intersects that shortest-path mask.

Key insight
- Shortest paths identify the route through open connector corridors, while the wall-bordered square rooms need to be expanded from the route centerline to their full interior.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="e729b7be" lines="8-20">
<description>
Complete the scene by adding the point-reflected copy of the colored object across the central marker.

Input structure
- The grid has odd dimensions, a dominant background, and a center-row marker color appearing only at the left edge, center, and right edge.
- A single divider color fills the center column except for the center marker.
- All remaining foreground pixels form the object to copy, entirely on the left side of the divider.
Rule
- Keep the input unchanged.
- For every object pixel, paint the pixel rotated 180 degrees around the center marker with the same color.
Key insight
- The center marker is the rotation center; the yellow-looking divider is not mirrored, it only separates the source from the empty target side.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="b745798f" lines="18-33">
<description>
Expand each 3-pixel L marker into a same-color corner L on an empty background.

Input structure
- The grid is an odd square with one dominant background color.
- Every foreground object is a monochromatic 4-connected 3-pixel L inside a
  2x2 box.

Rule
- Clear the grid to the background.
- For each marker, map its L orientation to the corresponding outer corner
  and draw that corner L in the marker color with arm length half the grid.

Key insight
- The small marker is a miniature corner; its orientation, not its position,
  chooses which outer corner receives the expanded L.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="aa62e3f4" lines="18-35">
<description>
Replace the outlined object with the outside contour of that outline in the marker color.

Input structure:
- A dominant background surrounds one 8-connected foreground outline.
- The outline is the unique largest foreground color.
- One or more smaller interior colors are inside the outline; the marker color
  is the unique smallest non-outline color.

Rule:
- Erase all original foreground pixels to background.
- Paint every background pixel that is 4-adjacent to the outline with the
  marker color.
- Keep the grid shape unchanged.

Key insight: The colored interior marker supplies the paint color, while the
largest outline supplies only the contour whose outside edge is traced.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="17829a00" lines="21-36">
<description>
Move each colored object vertically until it touches the matching top or bottom border bar.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- The top row is a full-width bar in one foreground color and the bottom row is a full-width bar in another.
- All other foreground pixels are 8-connected monochrome objects whose color matches one of those bars.

Rule:
- Top-bar-colored objects move upward so their bbox top is row 1.
- Bottom-bar-colored objects move downward so their bbox bottom is the row above the bottom bar.
- A downward-moving one-column segment whose landing overlaps its source becomes the continuous vertical segment swept between them.

Key insight:
The horizontal bars are destination anchors: color determines which border an object slides toward, while columns and object shape are otherwise preserved.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="a09f6c25" lines="33-50">
<description>
Recolor each non-singleton red object by its symmetry axis and erase isolated red pixels.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color and red 4-connected foreground objects.
- Every non-singleton red object has exactly one reflection symmetry category:
  horizontal, vertical, or diagonal.
- Single red pixels are noise.

Rule:
- Start from an all-background output of the same shape.
- Recolor horizontally symmetric red objects blue, vertically symmetric objects
  green, and diagonally symmetric objects pink.
- Leave isolated red pixels as background.

Key insight: The target color is not tied to object position or size; it is a
label for the object's reflection symmetry.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>


